{
  "image_name": "4fc74f6ed671ee79bc3795cb2ad6a1c3.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the life cycle of a messenger RNA from its birth in the nucleus to its use in the cytoplasm, emphasizing that many proteins bind the RNA at each stage. During transcription, RNA polymerase II produces a pre‑mRNA from DNA while processing factors assemble on the transcript. Visible examples include the cap-binding complex at the 5′ end, splicing factors and the spliceosome along the RNA, and 3′ end cleavage/polyadenylation factors near the polymerase, showing that capping, splicing, and end formation occur co-transcriptionally.\n\nAfter processing, the mRNA is packaged for nuclear export. The diagram shows export-associated proteins such as TREX, exon junction complexes left after splicing, and a nucleo-cytoplasmic shuttling protein, indicating that the mature RNA carries a protein signature as it crosses from nucleus to cytoplasm.\n\nIn the cytoplasm, these bound factors help control translation and mRNA stability. The mRNA is shown circularized, with poly(A)-binding proteins near one end and translation initiation factors recruiting a ribosome to the start codon, labeled AUG. Additional ribosomes, elongation factors, and regulatory proteins appear on the transcript, while other factors promote either degradation or stabilization. Overall, the figure presents mRNA as a dynamically packaged molecule whose associated proteins coordinate processing, export, translation, and turnover.",
